CORE no-new-smt
main.c
--little-endian --unwind 6 --unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ assertion k == 4: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ assertion r\.s\.data\[1\] == 'b': SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ assertion k == 7: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ assertion w\.s\.data\[1\] == 'y': SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ assertion u\.h == 0x0702: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ assertion u\.b\[2\] == 9: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^\[main\.unwind\.\d+\]
--
Writing some bytes of a union (memmove into one member's array) must keep
the values that the other bytes have in each member: field sensitivity
computed every member's new value from the fields of the widest member, so
the constant size stored in the short member was lost and the loop bound
became symbolic (an unwinding assertion).  The short member is as wide as the
union in r and narrower than the union in w.  The last part writes bytes of a
union with a value that reads other bytes of the same union.
